6. Feature requirements
6.1 Mailbox connection
Gmail — OAuth2 via Gmail API (gmail.send, gmail.readonly scopes only)
Outlook / Microsoft 365 — OAuth2 via Microsoft Graph API
Zoho Mail — OAuth2 via Zoho Mail API (preferred) with SMTP/IMAP as fallback
Any other provider — generic SMTP (send) + IMAP (read/reply/bounce detection)
Test-connection flow required before saving any mailbox: send a test email + verify inbox read access
Store all credentials encrypted at rest (KMS-backed); never log plaintext credentials
Detect and surface reconnect prompts when OAuth refresh tokens are revoked or expire
In-app guided setup for app-password generation (Gmail/Outlook) with screenshots
6.2 DNS / deliverability setup wizard
Auto-generate SPF, DKIM, and DMARC TXT records for the client's sending domain
"Verify" button that checks DNS propagation and record correctness
Flag misconfigured or missing records with plain-language fix instructions
Custom tracking domain (CNAME) per client/workspace — no shared tracking domain across tenants
6.3 Warmup engine
New mailboxes enter a mandatory warmup queue before being eligible for live campaign sending
Ramp schedule: start at low daily volume, increase gradually over 2–4 weeks toward target sending cap
Warmup traffic routed through dedicated warmup-only domains, isolated from client campaign-sending domains
Behavioral simulation: randomized send delays, open simulation, occasional replies, spam-folder recovery (move-to-inbox when landed in spam)
Content variation across warmup emails (no identical templates) to avoid pattern detection
Per-mailbox health score (cold / warming / warm) visible on a dashboard
Auto-graduation: mailbox becomes eligible for live sends once health score and ramp duration thresholds are met (manual override available)
6.4 Campaign / sequence builder
Multi-step sequences: email → wait (configurable) → conditional branch (opened / clicked / replied / no reply) → next step
A/B variant support per step (subject + body)
AI-generated personalized variants per lead, pulling from existing Magnivo research-agent output (not just merge tags)
Campaign attaches to a mailbox pool, not a single mailbox — sends rotate across the pool to keep per-mailbox volume low
Per-mailbox and per-pool daily send caps, business-hours + timezone-aware scheduling
6.5 List management & hygiene
CSV import with dedup against existing leads
Email verification (syntax + MX + catch-all detection) before a lead enters a sequence
Suppression list (org-wide do-not-contact), auto-added on hard bounce or unsubscribe
One-click unsubscribe via List-Unsubscribe header (RFC 8058), required per Google/Yahoo bulk sender rules — not just an in-body link
6.6 Unified inbox
Replies from all connected mailboxes surfaced in one view
AI-based reply classification: interested / not interested / OOO / unsubscribe request / needs-human-review
Suggested reply drafts generated by Magnivo agents
Auto-pause sequence enrollment on any reply or bounce for that lead
6.7 Analytics & monitoring
Per-mailbox and per-campaign: sent, delivered, opened, clicked, replied, bounced, unsubscribed
Bounce classification: hard (auto-suppress permanently) vs soft (retry with backoff; auto-pause mailbox if soft-bounce rate crosses threshold)
Spam complaint rate tracking via Google Postmaster Tools / Microsoft SNDS integration; auto-pause mailbox if complaint rate exceeds 0.3%
Domain/mailbox reputation score, blacklist monitoring
6.8 Multi-tenancy & workspace structure
Hierarchy: Workspace → Sub-accounts (optional, for agency-style clients) → Mailboxes
Per-tenant sending isolation: one client's list/domain issues must not affect another tenant's shared infra (warmup pool, tracking domains)
Usage counters per mailbox (sends, opens, etc.) tracked from day one, even before billing is wired up
7. Technical requirements
Sending layer: queue-based dispatcher (job queue, e.g. BullMQ/Sidekiq-style or Temporal for durable multi-day ramp workflows), per-mailbox rate limiting, retry/backoff on transient SMTP errors
Tracking layer: self-hosted open-pixel and click-redirect service on per-client tracking domains
Reply/bounce detection: Gmail push notifications (Pub/Sub) and Microsoft Graph webhooks preferred over polling; IMAP polling as fallback for generic SMTP mailboxes
Compliance: CAN-SPAM/GDPR-compliant footer, unsubscribe handling, suppression list enforced at the send layer (not just UI-level)
Security: encrypted credential storage, minimum-necessary OAuth scopes, refresh-token rotation handling
